    <string name="onboarding_1_title">What are lucid dreams?</string>
    <string name="onboarding_1_body">A lucid dream is a state where you are asleep but aware that you are dreaming. In that moment you can control what happens: fly, change your surroundings, meet anyone you like.\n\nThis is not fiction — it is a real phenomenon studied by neuroscientists. About 55% of people have experienced a lucid dream at least once. With practice it becomes an ordinary skill.</string>
    <string name="onboarding_2_title">Why record your dreams?</string>
    <string name="onboarding_2_body">The brain forgets 90% of a dream within the first 10 minutes after waking. A dream journal is your main tool.\n\n• Trains dream memory: the more you write, the more you remember\n• Helps you notice recurring images and themes\n• Teaches you to recognise the \"signs\" that you are dreaming — the key to lucidity\n• Over time you will start seeing patterns that lead to lucid dreams</string>
    <string name="onboarding_3_title">Locations — your dream map</string>
    <string name="onboarding_3_body">Many people return again and again to the same places in their dreams: an old house, an unfamiliar city, a forest, a school.\n\nLocations help you:\n• Collect these places in one spot\n• See how often you visit them\n• Notice connected dreams and build your own \"dream map\"\n\nWhen you recognise a familiar place in a dream — it is a powerful trigger for lucidity.</string>
    <string name="onboarding_4_title">Ready to start?</string>
    <string name="onboarding_4_body">All you need is to wake up and write immediately. Even fragments, even one word.\n\nAfter a week of regular entries you will notice that you remember more. After a month — the first lucid moments.\n\nYour journey to lucid dreaming starts right now. 🌙</string>
